## What the research says

Spa treatment for metabolic complaints is not merely tradition — but nor is it yet a field with strong evidence. A review of 22 balneotherapy studies in diabetes and obesity links both bathing and drinking sulphate and bicarbonate waters with lower blood glucose and body weight, yet only three of those studies had more than a hundred participants ([Maeda et al., 2026](https://consensus.app/papers/details/4f3bc4fade0e5c85a19be59f75baf233/), *Int J Biometeorol*). The firmest support concerns what the spa offers as a framework: in a French randomised trial with 257 participants, body mass index had fallen one year after a three-week spa programme by 1.9 points, compared with 0.2 under usual care ([Hanh et al., 2012](https://consensus.app/papers/details/7dbe44960ff55b5eb0b27cd970c93926/), *Evid Based Complement Alternat Med*; only participants who completed the programme were assessed). And in a controlled study of 340 patients, weight loss was maintained at eleven months only by those who received education alongside the spa cure ([Schnebelen-Berthier et al., 2019](https://consensus.app/papers/details/ce94b07d6802580283fee76ae74a0cf6/), *Obes Res Clin Pract*; the study was not randomised). **The spa is therefore a safe complement to standard care, not a replacement for it** — and its strength lies in the regime, not in any single treatment.

It is also striking how little can be enough: a meta-analysis of four randomised trials with 454 overweight patients with knee osteoarthritis showed that joint function improves significantly once weight falls by more than **five per cent** ([Christensen et al., 2007](https://doi.org/10.1136/ard.2006.065904), *Ann Rheum Dis*). For such a goal, a spa cure offers good starting conditions — movement, regularity, medical supervision and a dietary regime under one roof.

But it is only fair to say what the spa cannot do: it does not replace a lasting change of diet and exercise at home. It is the starting line, not the finish.

## The cure as a reset of habits

Why does spa tradition speak of **three weeks**? Because that is roughly the span in which the body adapts to a new rhythm — regular meals, regular movement, a regular drinking regime — and new habits begin to settle. For stays covered by Czech public health insurance, the length is set by the indication list and the referring physician's proposal: for diabetes (IV/1) it is 21 days, and 21 or 14 days for a repeat stay. Self-paying guests most often choose one to three weeks, agreed with the spa physician — shorter stays are more restorative in character, while the classic three-week cure gives the metabolism, and the mind, the most room to "switch over".

That is where the real strength of the cure lies: it is not about the water or any single treatment, but about a whole connected regime — [the movement that, in a spa town, lengthens life](/en/magazine/movement-spa-extends-life), balanced food, calm for the nervous system, and expert guidance. At home it is hard to keep all of that together; at the spa you receive it as one package.

## Who the metabolic cure suits

The metabolic cure is valued above all by people whose excess weight is bound up with metabolic disorders, by those with early type 2 diabetes, gout or digestive complaints — and by anyone seeking a trustworthy, medically led start to a change of lifestyle.

Conversely, it always holds that whether the cure is suitable, which springs apply and what dose of the drinking cure is appropriate is judged by the spa physician according to your state of health. Some conditions — acute illness, or certain serious heart and kidney diseases, for example — may rule a cure out or alter it considerably. The first step of a metabolic cure therefore leads not to a spring, but to the consulting room.

And that is precisely the difference between a fashionable diet and a spa cure: the diet promises you a number on the scales; the spa offers you a path along which that number can be approached safely, and for good.
